From 7db64dd646da47b4a3f19a296567dd472f9dedea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 21:04:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 62/76] rc3-instr 6/8: validate req_idx in the block-table
 gather and the slot mapping  (Luna guards 3 and 4)

Two kernels in vllm/v1/worker/gpu/block_table.py take a request row out of a
device-side mapping and use it to index a persistent, max_num_reqs-row table
with no bound:

  _gather_block_tables_kernel   req_idx    = idx_mapping[batch_idx]
  _compute_slot_mappings_kernel req_state_idx = idx_mapping[batch_idx]

An out-of-range row does not fault on its own in the gather -- it copies
another request's (or no request's) block ids into this step's working table,
and every kernel downstream then dereferences those ids. That is the quiet
version of the S2 failure: the illegal address surfaces later, in attention,
with metadata that looks internally consistent.

Both bounds come from an argument the launcher already has. The gather bound is
num_blocks_stride, which IS max_num_reqs, so it needs no new argument; the slot
mapping takes the same value as a new bt_num_rows.

Fail-soft behaviour follows what each kernel already does for a padded row:
  * gather: zero the destination row and return (the padded-row path above).
  * slot mapping: write PAD_ID over this request's token range and return, so
    no downstream kernel sees a stale slot id.

A second bound in the gather covers num_blocks itself: a count wider than the
table would run the copy loop past the end of both the source and destination
rows. It is clamped rather than skipped, so a too-large count still copies the
part that is real.

Launch-site audit: one launch each (block_table.py:166 and :204), both in this
file, no warmup registration and no AMD mirror.

ROUND 3: _GUARD_BIAS is bound as tl.constexpr. Round 2 declared it as a plain
module-level int, which a @triton.jit body cannot read, and the GPU smoke
failed every guarded-kernel test with "NameError: Cannot access global variable
_GUARD_BIAS". Checked the other module-level names in this file: none are
referenced from a jit body.

ROUND 9 -- this commit killed rc3 boot attempt 3, in warmup, at the first real
gather:

  triton CompilationError at 45:4
  AssertionError('initial value for `num_blocks` is of type int32[],
                  but the then block redefines it as int64[]')

`num_blocks` is loaded int32 from num_blocks_ptr; `max_num_blocks` is `stride`,
loaded from block_table_strides, which is int64. Clamping inside the `if`
rebound an int32 name to int64, and Triton requires a name to keep its type
across a branch.

The clamp is now UNCONDITIONAL with an explicit .to(tl.int32), and only the
recording stays inside the branch. That is better than a cast inside the `if`
twice over: there is no longer a branch-local redefinition of a live name at
all, which is the failure CLASS rather than this instance, and it matches the
shape every other guard in the series already uses -- clamp always, record when
the value was bad.

Why nothing caught it: the compile gate listed this kernel under "left to the
suites", and tests/v1/worker/gpu/ contained no block-table test at all, so it
was left to nobody and its first compile was the first forward of a four-rank
boot. Both halves are fixed -- the gate now launches gather, slot mapping and
the zeroer at real shapes, and tests/v1/worker/gpu/test_block_table_guards.py
launches the real kernels and checks both the legal path (counters must stay
at zero) and the guarded path (counter fires, destination row zeroed, the
neighbouring rows untouched).

Draft for Astra review. Not merged into any RC.
